WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.257
This example groups related helper functions together.

00:00:03.257 --> 00:00:06.672
We define get_profit, get_profit_margin, and format_percent.

00:00:06.672 --> 00:00:12.087
These functions work together like a small metrics module.

00:00:12.087 --> 00:00:16.097
The get_profit function calculates sales minus costs.

00:00:16.097 --> 00:00:19.347
The get_profit_margin function calls get_profit and divides the result by sales.

00:00:19.347 --> 00:00:27.077
The format_percent function turns a decimal value into a readable percent string.

00:00:27.077 --> 00:00:31.182
Then we set sales and costs, call the helper functions, and print the profit and profit margin.

00:00:31.182 --> 00:00:36.597
This example is still written in one cell, but conceptually it behaves like a module.

00:00:36.597 --> 00:00:43.972
The functions are related and could easily be moved into a file such as metrics_tools.py.

00:00:43.972 --> 00:00:45.717
This is an important habit in analytics.

00:00:45.717 --> 00:00:53.932
If you have several helper functions that support one part of the workflow, group them together.

00:00:53.932 --> 00:00:57.622
That way, the main analysis stays easier to read.

00:00:57.622 --> 00:01:01.802
After running the code, change the sales or costs values.

00:01:01.802 --> 00:01:06.934
You can also change the format_percent function to show two decimal places instead of one.
